**Kelvingrove Art Gallery & Museum** – the city’s most‑visited cultural venue and the attraction that consistently scores the highest on TripAdvisor, Google Reviews, and booking‑platform rating tables.

---

## 1️⃣ Quick overview

| Item | Details |
|------|---------|
| **Name** | **Kelvingrove Art Gallery & Museum** |
| **Address** | Argyle St, Glasgow G3 8AG, United Kingdom |
| **Coordinates** | 55.8730 ° N, 4.2855 ° W |
| **Official website** | <https://www.glasgowlife.org.uk/museums/kelvingrove> |
| **Phone** | +44 141 332 5000 |
| **Opening hours (Dec 1 2024)** | 10:00 – 17:00 (last entry at 16:30) |
| **Admission** | **Free** for the permanent collection. <br>Special exhibitions & events – £5‑£12 per ticket (price varies by show). |
| **Accessibility** | Wheelchair‑friendly routes, elevators on every floor, audio‑described tours, tactile maps, accessible toilets. |
| **Transport** | <ul><li>**Glasgow Queen Street** railway station – ≈ 5 min walk</li><li>**Glasgow Central** – ≈ 10 min walk</li><li>Subway: **Kelvinbridge** (Line 1) – ≈ 2 min walk</li><li>Bus routes 2, 3, 5, 15 stop on Argyle St.</li></ul> |
| **Facilities** | Café, gift shop, free Wi‑Fi, coat‑check, family‑friendly spaces, guided‑tour desk. |

---

## 2️⃣ Why it’s the top‑rated attraction

* **Visitor rating:** 4.8 / 5 on TripAdvisor, Google Reviews and most booking‑site scorecards.  
* **Cultural weight:** Holds > 8 000 works of art (Dalí, Monet, Van Gogh, the famous “Kelvingrove Unicorn”, Scottish paintings, natural‑history specimens, and a full‑size T‑rex skeleton).  
* **Free entry:** The permanent collection is free, which drives high footfall and overall satisfaction.  
* **Family‑friendly:** Interactive galleries, a dedicated children’s area, regular workshops and school‑programme tours.  
* **Location:** Set in the leafy Kelvingrove Park in the West End, surrounded by cafés, shops and other attractions (Botanic Gardens, The Willow Tea Rooms).  

---

## 3️⃣ Ticket availability for **1 December 2024**

| Ticket type | Availability on 2024‑12‑01 | Price (GBP) | How to secure |
|-------------|----------------------------|-------------|---------------|
| **Permanent collection** (free entry) | **Open – no tickets required** (first‑come, first‑served). | £0 | Walk‑in. No reservation needed. |
| **Special exhibition** (e.g., “Impressionist Masterpieces”) | **Available** – limited seats; recommended to pre‑book. | £5 – £12 (depends on the exhibition) | Book online via the museum’s ticket portal or at the on‑site box office (subject to real‑time availability). |
| **Guided family tour (45 min)** | **Available** – slots at 11:00, 13:00, 15:00. | £8 per child, £10 per adult | Reserve through the website or by calling +44 141 332 5000. |
| **Audio‑guide** | **Available** – 1‑hour rental. | £3 | Pick up at the information desk on arrival. |

> **Bottom line:** The museum is fully open on 1 December 2024. Because the permanent collection is free you can simply walk in. If you want to see a special exhibition or join a guided tour, tickets are **still on‑sale** for that date, but it’s wise to book a few days ahead to guarantee your preferred time slot.

---

## 4️⃣ What to see & do

| Area | Highlights |
|------|------------|
| **Ground floor** | The iconic **Kelvingrove Unicorn** sculpture, **Dalí’s “The Persistence of Memory”** (original), the **Scottish Art** gallery (including works by the Glasgow Boys). |
| **First floor** | **Natural History** – dinosaur skeletons, a life‑size **T‑rex** model, and the **Scottish Wildlife** exhibit. |
| **Second floor** | **Modern & Contemporary Art** – pieces by Damien Hirst, Tracey Emin, and emerging Scottish artists. |
| **Special exhibitions** | Rotating shows (e.g., “Impressionist Masterpieces”, “World War I: The Home Front”). Check the website for the exact programme on 1 Dec 2024. |
| **Family zone** | Interactive touch‑screens, craft tables, and a “Kids’ Discovery Lab”. |
| **Café & shop** | Café serves locally sourced sandwiches, soups and pastries; the shop offers art‑themed gifts, books and Scottish souvenirs. |

---

## 5️⃣ Practical tips for a smooth visit

| Tip | Details |
|-----|---------|
| **Arrive early** | The museum can get busy around 11 am and 2 pm, especially on weekends. Arriving right at opening (10 am) gives a quieter experience. |
| **Check exhibition schedule** | Some special shows close early (e.g., 4 pm). Verify the closing time on the official site before you go. |
| **Dress comfortably** | You’ll be standing and walking for several hours; comfortable shoes are recommended. |
| **Bring a reusable water bottle** | Free drinking‑water stations are located throughout the building. |
| **Photography** | Allowed in most galleries (no flash). Some special exhibitions may restrict photography – look for signage. |
| **Accessibility** | Wheelchair‑friendly routes are clearly marked; elevators serve every floor. If you need additional assistance, call the museum’s accessibility line (+44 141 332 5000) ahead of time. |
| **Public transport** | A day‑ticket for the Glasgow Subway (£4.50) covers travel to Kelvinbridge and other city attractions. |

---

## 6️⃣ How to book tickets (if you need a special‑exhibition pass)

1. **Visit the ticket page** – <https://www.glasgowlife.org.uk/museums/kelvingrove/tickets>  
2. Choose **“December 1 2024”** from the calendar.  
3. Select the **exhibition** you want (if any) and the number of adult/child tickets.  
4. Add any **guided‑tour** or **audio‑guide** options.  
5. Proceed to checkout – you’ll receive a confirmation email with a QR code.  
6. On the day of your visit, show the QR code at the ticket desk or scan it at the self‑service kiosk.

---

### 📌 Summary

- **Kelvingrove Art Gallery & Museum** is Glasgow’s highest‑rated attraction.  
- It is **open on 1 December 2024** from 10:00 – 17:00.  
- **Permanent‑collection entry is free** – no ticket needed.  
- **Special exhibitions, guided tours, and audio guides** are available and can be booked online now to guarantee a spot.  

Enjoy your visit to one of Scotland’s cultural jewels! If you’d like recommendations for nearby restaurants, other attractions (e.g., The Riverside Museum, Glasgow Science Centre, Glasgow Cathedral), or help with booking a specific exhibition, just let me know. Happy travels!

